% DYK-BEGIN
\begin{didyouknow}{Death by Horse Kick}
In 1898 Ladislaus Bortkiewicz showed that the yearly number of Prussian cavalrymen killed by horse kicks, recorded across army corps over 20 years, closely followed a Poisson distribution. Rare, independent events spread over many opportunities produce Poisson counts, whatever their cause. His book, \emph{The Law of Small Numbers}, made this grim dataset one of the most famous examples in statistics.
\end{didyouknow}
% DYK-END

% DYK-BEGIN
\begin{didyouknow}{Sunspots and Memory}
In 1927 the statistician George Udny Yule modeled yearly sunspot numbers by letting each year's value depend on the previous two years plus random shocks. This was the first autoregressive model, and it showed how a correlated random sequence can mimic periodic behavior. Autocorrelation measures how strongly a sequence remembers its own past, and it is the tool for telling real rhythms from noise.
\end{didyouknow}
% DYK-END

% DYK-BEGIN
\begin{didyouknow}{Random Matrices Began in Statistics}
Random matrices first appeared in statistics, not physics. In 1928 John Wishart derived the distribution of sample covariance matrices built from Gaussian data, now called the Wishart distribution. In the 1950s Eugene Wigner used random matrices to model nuclear energy levels, and in the 1970s they turned up in the Riemann zeros. The same mathematics serves surveys, nuclei and primes.
\end{didyouknow}
% DYK-END

