\begin{beyond}
\textbf{Taken on trust: continuous pairs.} For two continuous variables the joint distribution is a \emph{joint density} $f(x,y)$, and probabilities of rectangles are iterated integrals, $P(a<X<b\text{ and }c<Y<d)=\int_a^b\Bigl[\int_c^d f(x,y)\,\dd y\Bigr]\dd x$: first integrate over $y$ with $x$ held fixed, then integrate the result over $x$. Sums over $j$ and $k$ become such integrals, the density of $X$ alone is $f_X(x)=\int f(x,y)\,\dd y$, and independence means $f(x,y)=f_X(x)f_Y(y)$. With these replacements every result of this section holds by the same steps; the justification that iterated integrals may be split and reordered like sums is in \cite{rhb}. We use the continuous results only in the same form as the discrete ones.
\end{beyond}

For independent variables the covariance term vanishes and variances simply add. This one fact drives the whole of \cref{sec:probability-sampling}.

\paragraph{Correlation coefficient of two random variables.} For random variables with $\sigma_X>0$ and $\sigma_Y>0$, the \emph{correlation coefficient} is
\begin{equation}
\rho=\frac{\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)}{\sigma_X\sigma_Y}.
\label{eq:probability-rho}
\end{equation}
For the empirical distribution of two paired lists (each pair $(x_i,y_i)$ picked with probability $1/n$), $\mathrm{Cov}$ becomes the list covariance and $\rho$ becomes the Pearson coefficient $r$ defined below. The proof of $\abs{r}\le1$ given below also proves $\abs{\rho}\le1$: replace $\sum u_iv_i$ by $E[X'Y']$, $\sum u_i^2$ by $E[X'^2]$ and $\sum v_i^2$ by $E[Y'^2]$; the proof uses only that the expectation of a square is nonnegative and that expectation is linear.

A stronger version also holds.
\begin{beyond}
\textbf{Taken on trust: the strong law of large numbers.} For independent, identically distributed draws with finite mean, $\bar x\to\mu$ with probability 1, not merely with probability approaching 1. Its proof needs measure-theoretic probability and is beyond this book; we never use it.
\end{beyond}
